WHO;
WHO
Recent joint initiatives by the WHO and its collaborators have supported and promoted the advancement and validation of the EWARS tool to improve its prediction ability and design for larger-scale applications – particularly in data-poor settings and among unskilled users. The newly developed EWARSPlus model has additional features to support experts and unskilled users performing early descriptive measurements to ensure data reliability and usefulness for prediction purposes. Essentially, the EWARSPlus tool requires climate information (indicators) for the prediction of climate-sensitive disease outbreaks and stage response. This initiative is piloted into four districts namely Zomba, Chikwawa, Salima and Chitipa. The training also covered the equiped the health workers on adressing the impacts of climate change and health. 30 health care workers were trained in EWARS in each district, totaling to 120.
